From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 19:33:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] editor overlay: distance adaptive slope view and native build
 grid (quadtree LOD), ground overlays depth tested

Slope view: the blocks are a quadtree (EdOverlayLod.h): 64 cell roots over the visible rectangle, each cut into four while its longest projected edge exceeds 16 px
(merged below 11 px, hysteresis state per block between frames), down to single cells; nearest blocks first, leaf budget 40000, quad budget 120000. The ground under the
camera no longer gets the block size of the horizon. Projected corners live in a hash cache (NodeTable) shared by neighbouring blocks and kept while the camera stands still;
a block next to a finer neighbour grows 1 px over that edge; tiles are rated nearest the camera first; ed_overlay_perf gets leaves / levels.

Build grid: ed_overlay_grid(x0, z0, x1, z1 [, rgb, alpha]) / ed_overlay_grid_off, drawn natively from the outlines of the leaves of a second quadtree (every cell where a cell
is >10 px, every 2^L cells farther away, alpha fading with distance, every line once, DrapeEdge follows the ground). No Lua line rebuild.

Occlusion: the overlay vertices were z = 0 under a no-depth technique (x-ray through hills). They now carry the depth of their ground point moved towards the camera by
0.25 + 0.008 x distance units (BiasedDepth / VertexZ) and are drawn with the new technique TEdGroundOverlay (depth test, no writes); ed_overlay_depth(false) turns it off.

Tests: EdOverlayLodTest (new), EdOverlayDrapeTest (DrapeEdge), EdOverlaySlopeTest (Store::Version).

